I look at my mother on the screen.
She asks about my first weeks in January.
I tell her about a long morning.
"It was a very cold and boring Tuesday," I say.
I took the bus into the city's citizen center.
There were really many people in the large room.
I pulled a number from a small machine.
My number was seventy-two.
But the screen on the wall only showed thirteen.
The queue was incredibly slow.
I sat on a hard chair and waited for two hours.
I drank three cups of bad coffee meanwhile.
Finally, a friendly man called my number.
I stood up and walked over to his desk.
I gave him my passport and my documents from the software company.
He read the papers and typed on his computer.
I finally got my private registration number.
He asked me to take really good care of the important number.
"That does not sound very fun," my mother smiles.
